Title: "Overview"
Id: "4d980f35-73f3-4400-a0d0-ae79e2a7db57"

Text: "The definition-like top-level groups are `Defines:`, `Declares:`, `Refines:`,
and `States:`. Each is backed by a command heading, introduces that command's
signature, and may carry the support sections shown below.

```mlg-fragment
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
```

Which one to use:

- `Defines:` introduces a mathematical form or a typed, value-bearing
  declaration. It is the only kind with a `declares:` section and the only kind
  that may use an infix-specification heading (`[A \\:subset:/ B]`).
- `Declares:` introduces a command by an `is` or specification statement. It has
  an `expresses:` section, and it is the kind a `Requires:` `definition:` item
  may refer to.
- `Refines:` defines a refined command in terms of another command. It is the
  only kind that may use a refined command header (`::`).
- `States:` defines a named statement with a required `that:` body.

`Defines:`, `Declares:`, and `States:` must include at least one of `called:` or
`written:` in `Documented:`. `Refines:` must instead include `adjective:`, and
its `Documented:` rejects `called:`.

`Provides:` and its nested `symbol:` were renamed to `Enables:` and
`capability:`. `Requires:` is used for definitional capabilities or required
definitions, while `Enables:` is used for additional supported notation; for
type checking the ordinary capabilities of both are combined.

`Justification:` replaces the older `Justified:` section, and every non-prose
top-level item may carry its own `Writing:` section after `Aliases:`."
Id: "1764c7bb-3657-4005-9490-4847e48be174"
